// The one surface that is visible when the panel window is not in front.
//
// It answers a question the window cannot answer while it is buried under other
// windows: is Squid running, and what is it doing? The icon says the first, the
// hover text says the second in the status line's own words.
//
// **It deliberately does not turn "close" into "put away".** A visible
// `SystemTrayIcon` keeps Slint's event loop alive by itself, so hiding the window
// could have meant the process survives — which is what a tray is usually for. It
// does not here, and the reason is measured rather than cautious: on Linux, Slint
// 1.17.1 publishes the tray's dbusmenu object path and then exports an *empty*
// menu. A fake `org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher` pointed at the running panel read
// `GetLayout` back as `(0, (0, {}, []))`, with `AboutToShow` answering `false`,
// and it stayed empty with the simplest possible two-item menu. So on the platform
// this is developed on, a menu entry is not a way out — and a hidden window plus a
// live tray whose menu is empty is a process a person cannot quit from its own
// interface. Closing the window still ends the run, exactly as it did before.
//
// That leaves `Quit Squid` as the menu's only entry, and it earns its place on the
// host where the menu works: quitting without first hunting for a window that is
// behind six others. `Show Squid` is absent for the same reason it would be
// pointless — the window is never hidden while this process lives — and a
// permanently disabled entry is worse than no entry.
//
// Two things also deliberately absent. **Pause** stays in the panel's Recording
// section, which owns the prose that goes with it: pause control is FIFO-backed, so
// it is unconfigured on most runs and unavailable on Windows entirely. **A count**
// belongs to the desktop notice — only "Waiting for you" earns a badge in this
// design, and a Windows tray icon cannot carry one.
export component SquidTray inherits SystemTrayIcon {
    // `icon` and `tooltip` belong to `SystemTrayIcon`, and Slint generates Rust
    // accessors only for what the exported component declares — an inherited
    // property has no setter on the binding. So each one gets a declared property
    // here and the inherited one is bound to it. The names differ because they
    // must: a re-declaration would collide with the base rather than override it.
    in property <image> icon-image;
    in property <string> hover-text;
    icon: root.icon-image;
    tooltip: root.hover-text;

    // A literal, unlike the tooltip, because this one is not state: `title` is what
    // accessibility tools read and what some desktops show when they list tray
    // icons. It was empty until a fake StatusNotifier host read the properties back
    // off the running panel. Windows renders only the icon and ignores it; that is
    // no reason to publish nothing on the host where a screen reader is the only
    // way to find this icon at all.
    title: "Squid";

    callback quit();

    Menu {
        MenuItem {
            title: "Quit Squid";
            activated => { root.quit(); }
        }
    }
}
